Participants noted that inflation had continued to ease over the intermeeting period, with both headline and core price measures moving closer to the Committee's two percent objective. Several participants cautioned that progress could be uneven and that services prices remained elevated relative to goods prices.

Labor market conditions were described as gradually cooling. Job openings declined, the pace of payroll gains slowed, and the unemployment rate edged up, although it remained low by historical standards. Participants judged that wage growth had moderated and no longer posed a significant upside risk to inflation.

Consumer spending remained resilient, supported by solid household balance sheets, though lower income households reported drawing down savings. Business fixed investment was little changed, and manufacturers reported softer new orders amid cautious demand.

In their discussion of monetary policy, participants agreed to maintain the target range for the federal funds rate at its current level. Most saw the policy stance as restrictive and well positioned to bring inflation sustainably back to target, and they emphasized that decisions would remain data dependent.

Participants discussed the outlook for the federal funds rate over the coming year. Many anticipated that rate reductions would become appropriate if inflation continued to decline and the labor market softened further, while a few noted that premature easing could stall progress on inflation.

Members reviewed inflation expectations, noting that longer-term measures from surveys and market-based indicators remained well anchored near two percent. Participants stressed the importance of keeping expectations anchored through clear communication about the policy path.


